#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161672 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "486f4292-2f50-5d89-be1a-b3c054b449e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10082" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two pills is drawn from a population. The population has pills of two different active ingredient concentrations, $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $\\textrm{15}\\,\\textrm{mg/ml},$ distributed in the ratio $1:2.$ Calculate $\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the pills in the population have concentrations of $30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}$ and $15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+2 = 3$ pills with only the following concentrations:\n\n$$\n30\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}, \\qquad 15\\,\\textrm{mg/ml}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(30+2\\cdot 15) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{3} \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{mg/ml},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({30}^{2}+2\\cdot {15}^{2})-{20}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(900+450)-400 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1\\,350-400 \\\\  & =450-400 \\\\  & =50\\,(\\text{mg/ml})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample size $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] =\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} = \\dfrac{50}{2} = 25\\,(\\textrm{mg/ml})^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161673 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/id #uuid "41713dda-2195-5bd5-b0be-941b8188cb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10083" :question/problem "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{200}$ is a sample of size $n=200$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu = 23$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = 4,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overline{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is $\\sigma^2 = 4$ and the sample size is $n=200,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{4}{200} \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{0.02} \\approx 0.14 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161674 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/id #uuid "0f59a118-79ce-5746-8917-6f4092dcbc6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10084" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ of $n$ coconuts is drawn from a population. The population has coconuts of two different weights, $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb},$ distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Determine the smallest sample size required to give a standard error of less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coconuts in the population have weights $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}$ and $2\\,\\textrm{lb},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $2:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $2+3 = 5$ coconuts with only the following weights:\n\n$$\n1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 1.5\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}, \\qquad 2\\,\\textrm{lb}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot 1.5+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =1.8\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2\\cdot {1.5}^{2}+3\\cdot {2}^{2})-{1.8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(16.5)-3.24 \\\\  & =3.3-3.24 \\\\  & =0.06\\,{\\text{lb}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma= \\sqrt{0.06},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{0.06}{ n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.02\\,\\textrm{lb}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.06}}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{{0.02}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.06}{0.0004}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{150} \\\\ n & >150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=151.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837d50fa-3624-53fb-8de4-8e39ada04ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "145" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159501} {:answer/id #uuid "53ba806b-60d2-5e3e-aa51-1fe1377d1595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159504} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3328ff-ae5d-5f61-b10f-59d82fd77a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159505} {:answer/id #uuid "0e26866e-c348-5c85-8477-d41e8ad68bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,501" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435294} {:answer/id #uuid "40b32199-db61-50a1-b590-f246ea88373a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,601" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40b32199-db61-50a1-b590-f246ea88373a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,601" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435299} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7faff91-b347-5cfd-b5ed-5befb5086c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f71d380-9e2a-56fd-91c4-3ba333fbded4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143205" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =12$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16,$ determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.1.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{16}=4,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.1.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{4}{0.1} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >40 \\\\ n & >{40}^{2} \\\\ n & >1\\,600.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=1\\,601.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0817bb7f-129c-5da6-94a6-8a21129fe1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435293} {:answer/id #uuid "615089ef-def0-59e6-8785-6915380e3305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435302} {:answer/id #uuid "cf72bf32-c5fb-5035-9bac-6d90253affbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435306} {:answer/id #uuid "f788c75b-6288-5270-a9e5-f4402ccef1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435308} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ea8bce-5714-5cd0-ba22-01b51246f8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0817bb7f-129c-5da6-94a6-8a21129fe1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435293} :answer-field/id #uuid "95ece8c6-b709-55fd-aec1-dac8fa69933c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "107c69d7-4c3f-52a6-a6d4-c51559524e04" :question/math-academy-id "q-143396" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{64}$ is a sample of size $n=64$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =10$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16,$ calculate the value of $\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16$ and the sample size $n=64,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{16}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18afdf58-dba2-58e9-a056-053f50fdcf13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435295} {:answer/id #uuid "4a87e415-1cae-5953-b9d0-0e4e16ef9fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435300} {:answer/id #uuid "71f70d07-afda-5c74-9287-7527216a5d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435303} {:answer/id #uuid "94fa21d1-285e-5bc0-b227-fce21b9d735a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435304} {:answer/id #uuid "c225523b-6878-5f1a-a71b-b222b61253ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94fa21d1-285e-5bc0-b227-fce21b9d735a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1495566f-30dc-5ca9-9171-d715fd9ce820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4f666bb-5559-5955-a86d-6b884c476741" :question/math-academy-id "q-143399" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{30}$ is a sample of size $n=30$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =-5$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9,$ calculate the value of $\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9$ and the sample size $n=30,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{9}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36470539-f464-546f-ba73-6270b016995f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435296} {:answer/id #uuid "39a9b6f2-d460-516e-a343-dad430e857a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435297} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef092e1-14dd-5d66-a852-0395cd09a641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435298} {:answer/id #uuid "5fffa88a-ab60-5e5a-ae9d-85cd50c46069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435301} {:answer/id #uuid "f7609979-80a6-5ca8-82ec-72fc4cf0ed29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7609979-80a6-5ca8-82ec-72fc4cf0ed29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435307} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0bec69d-d68a-518d-bb91-2fd951e08467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4ffdafd-ca0f-5aeb-9afa-3ab07c6f0225" :question/math-academy-id "q-143426" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{1000}$ is a sample of size $n=1\\,000$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =150$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=25,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=25$ and the sample size is $n=1\\,000,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{25}{1\\,000} \\\\  & =0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.025}≈0.16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397deda3-79ec-54be-8324-ca298ce871b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418424} {:answer/id #uuid "4d08f4df-3304-5238-9ee4-c8a784de2239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.78$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418425} {:answer/id #uuid "5de5b927-f54f-54d4-bb10-51cc65020f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.86$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418428} {:answer/id #uuid "b226dd71-89e3-53ba-931c-f7309d4db475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.22$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418437} {:answer/id #uuid "bb154b3d-66ec-5d69-b172-03533be8a6b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0.61$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b226dd71-89e3-53ba-931c-f7309d4db475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.22$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418437} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c53bd6-fdb5-5d5f-980b-becaa30267cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de928809-8125-57ca-bec0-eb6d36dc320c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143428" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ of four bills is drawn from a population. The population has bills of two different denominations, $$5$ and $$10,$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bills in the population have denominations $$5$ and $$10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bills only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$5,\\,$5,\\,$5,\\,$10\\,$10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(15+20) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =$7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {5}^{2}+2\\cdot {10}^{2})-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(275)-49 \\\\  & =55-49 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{6}{4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{1.5}≈$1.22.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7b64bf-b6ca-57e9-aaa1-181aed3d622b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425731} {:answer/id #uuid "160f3d12-5eab-5995-9f63-e81fe88512ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "241" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425733} {:answer/id #uuid "78a69e66-0db0-56b0-9e49-ac78e00b8276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "441" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425741} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a8088-2557-53ce-b8f2-d4dcab799771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "194" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425743} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7b1ee7-ec01-58b0-9c87-f819883b152a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae7b1ee7-ec01-58b0-9c87-f819883b152a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425745} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e022f4b-ff74-5796-8a0f-0137d56c7a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1b6912a-93b9-571b-8ea5-2d8ab50d8a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143486" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ bushes is drawn from a population. The population has bushes of two different heights, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.01\\,\\text{ft}.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bushes in the population have heights $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bushes only with the following heights:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\text{ft},\\,4\\,\\text{ft},\\,4\\,\\text{ft},\\,5\\,\\text{ft},\\,5\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(12+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5} \\\\  & =4.4\\,\\text{ft},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {4}^{2}+2\\cdot {5}^{2})-{4.4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(98)-19.36 \\\\  & =19.6-19.36 \\\\  & =0.24\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.24},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.01\\,\\text{ft}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{n}} & <0.01 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.24}}{0.01} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{{0.01}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{2\\,400} \\\\ n & >2\\,400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=2\\,401.$"}]}
